**Would I do it differently? Yes — the nested loop is the one thing I'd change.**

3,000 leads × 3,000 contacts is ~9 million inner iterations, each doing a `trim().toLowerCase()` comparison. In Apex that is on the order of tens of seconds of CPU, and it runs inside the import transaction, which shares the 10,000 ms synchronous CPU limit with everything else in that transaction (triggers, flows, the import's own DML). It will hit `System.LimitException: Apex CPU time limit exceeded`, and even at smaller volumes it quietly eats most of the budget. It also scales quadratically, so the "it's only a few thousand" assumption breaks the moment a bigger event or region comes along.

The fix costs nothing in readability: normalize each contact email once into a `Map<String, Id>` (keeping only the first contact per email, which preserves the "first in `contacts` order" rule), then do a single pass over the leads. That's O(n + m) ≈ 6,000 operations instead of 9,000,000, and the behaviour is identical: case-/whitespace-insensitive match, blank emails never match, one result per lead in lead order, no SOQL, no DML.

